This folder contains fingerprint-based tests for various models.

When a fingerprint test passes, that indicates that with very high
probability, the simulation has followed the same trajectory as when
the fingerprint was recorded, i.e. the times and modules of the events
were the same. That is, a passing fingerprint tests means that the
simulation model logic works the same as before.

However, a passing test does not guarantee that e.g. result recording
has not changed.

Fingerprints are fragile to NED changes and parameter value changes.

When a fingerprint test fails, the simulation's correctness has to be
verified by some other means, and the fingerprints in the tests
updated.


Tags
----

The sixth column of each CSV is a space-separated tag list. Tags exist so that
work on one subsystem has a fast inner loop instead of a full-suite round trip:

    ./fingerprints -m interference
    ./fingerprints -m 'propagation|bgcell'
    ./fingerprints -x idealchannel

-m and -x are Python regexes, matched against workingdir + " " + args + " " +
tags, so a tag that also occurs in a path or a config name selects both. The
tags below are chosen not to collide with anything in the current tree.

A tag says what a configuration exercises, not where it lives, because the
directory is already matchable. So there is no `d2d` tag -- the d2d rows are a
separate file, selectable as `./fingerprints simulations_d2d.csv` -- and no
`rlc` or `mec` tag either.

Every row carries exactly one of `radio` and `idealchannel`; the rest refine.

    radio          204  a stochastic channel model is in use, so path loss,
                        fading and shadowing are actually evaluated
    idealchannel    18  IdealChannelModel: reception is a coin flip and no
                        propagation is computed. These rows are radio-inert by
                        construction, and a change confined to the radio model
                        must leave all 18 byte-identical
    propagation     25  the configuration exists to exercise the propagation
                        matrix: the scenario x specification x LOS sweep of
                        simulations/channelmodel/, plus the two ChannelModel-*
                        configurations of simulations/nr/d2d/
    nofading        24  a stochastic model with both fading and shadowing
                        turned off, so the channel is geometry only. Like
                        idealchannel, but sensitive to path loss
    interference    37  downlinkInterference and/or uplinkInterference is
                        enabled, i.e. interference between real cells is
                        modeled. Interference from background cells is
                        bgcell, not this
    multicarrier    17  more than one component carrier, so the per-carrier
                        channelModel[] vector has more than one element
    bgcell          12  background cells are present (numBgCells > 0)
    bgue             8  background UEs are attached to a real cell. Background
                        UEs living inside a background cell are part of
                        bgcell, and a row can carry both
    anisotropic      6  an ANISOTROPIC antenna is configured somewhere, so the
                        horizontal antenna pattern is exercised

The tags were derived from each row's fully resolved configuration -- the
output of

    simu5g -u Cmdenv -f omnetpp.ini -c <Config> -r <Run> -q runconfig

which flattens the extends chain and resolves iteration variables -- combined
with the NED defaults of the parameters no .ini sets. They are not derived from
path names. Note that -q runconfig without -r dumps every run of the config
concatenated, which is a trap when a config iterates over a channel parameter.

Re-recording preserves tags: the runner rewrites only the fingerprint string,
in place, on the line it came from.


Notes on INET/OMNeT++ versions:

- **Fingerprints are INET/OMNeT++ version specific**: the fingerprints
  recorded in `simulations.csv` and `simulations_d2d.csv` are tied to the
  versions CI records them with, INET-4.7.0 and OMNeT++ 6.4.0 (see
  `.github/workflows/fingerprint-tests.yml`). Running the suite against a
  different INET or OMNeT++ version reports mismatches, and those mismatches
  do not indicate a problem.

- **What the version affects**: all 202 configurations have been run against
  INET-4.5.4, INET-4.6.0 and INET-4.7.0, under OMNeT++ 6.3.0 and 6.4.0;
  every configuration ran to completion in every combination, with no
  errors. The `~tNl` ingredient -- the packets that cross between network
  nodes, their times and their lengths -- is identical in all 202
  configurations in every combination tested, so the simulated network
  traffic itself is unaffected by the INET and OMNeT++ version. What moves
  is intra-node event scheduling (`tplx`, `tilx`) and recorded statistics
  (`sz`). Re-recording the baselines from INET-4.5.4 + OMNeT++ 6.3.0 to
  INET-4.7.0 + OMNeT++ 6.4.0 moved `sz` in all 202 configurations, `tilx`
  in 50 of them and `tplx` in 23, and `~tNl` in none. The steps that
  contribute:

  - **INET-4.5.4 to INET-4.6.0** is the substantial step: `sz` moves in all
    202 configurations, and `tplx` and `tilx` in 18 and 45 of them.

  - **INET-4.6.0 to INET-4.7.0** is nearly inert: 197 of the 202
    configurations are unchanged in all four ingredients. The five that
    differ -- `SchedulersTest` runs 0 and 12 in `simulations/lte/demo`, and
    `checkMulticastRange`, `D2DMulticast-1to2` and `D2DMulticast-1toM` in
    `simulations/lte/d2d_multicast` -- move only `tplx` and `tilx`, leaving
    both `sz` and `~tNl` untouched.

  - **OMNeT++ 6.3.0 to 6.4.0** moves no behavioral ingredient at all:
    `tplx`, `tilx` and `~tNl` are identical in all 202 configurations. Only
    `sz` moves, and only in the 181 configurations that record an empty
    statistic, because 6.3.0 writes the mean of an empty statistic as `-nan`
    while 6.4.0 writes `nan`, and the differing sign bit changes the hash.
    (6.4.0 also prints scalars with one more significant digit; the
    underlying values are the same, and fingerprints are computed from the
    values, not from the recorded text.)

- **Release and debug agree**: the release and the debug build produce the
  same fingerprints, which is why CI can check both against these baselines.
